WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.833
A rule needs two names
before an engine reads it.

00:00:02.833 --> 00:00:06.267
Now write the rule that matters
most, your idea of a good fit.

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:09.700
Today it lives in someone's head,
so every tool scores it differently.

00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:13.400
By the end you'll have a scoring model
you can read, test on a real account,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.733
and improve with outcomes.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.833
Mia calls Example Logistics a perfect fit.

00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:19.900
The CRM says tier C, the
sequencer says priority,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.567
and neither explains itself.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.833
Define fit once, test it on a
real account, read the reasons,

00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:26.333
and let outcomes improve it.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.867
Step one, who you sell to.

00:00:27.867 --> 00:00:30.733
A segment is a slice of the market
you work, by industry, size or region.

00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:34.100
A persona is a role you sell
to, with the titles that count.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.500
Both are definitions, so both
are signed and versioned.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.500
In the console: Setup, Your
definitions, the Pilot scope card.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.067
Personas arrive with your blueprint,
and change through a ChangeSet.

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:45.567
Step two, the weights sheet,
the heart of the model.

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:48.967
Each criterion, like industry or
company size, carries a weight,

00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:50.533
and together they make a fit score.

00:00:50.533 --> 00:00:52.733
Tiers turn scores into Hot, Warm and Cold.

00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:55.433
Thresholds decide what counts
as qualified or needs review.

00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:59.300
In the console: Setup, Your definitions,
the ICP and weights sheet card.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.567
It shows a drafted sheet with the
evidence behind each suggestion.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:04.300
Edit it, skip it, or approve it.

00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:06.500
One habit worth copying: start small.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.233
A few criteria you can defend to a
sales lead beat a long list you can't.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:13.267
Add one when the Learning
section shows evidence for it.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.533
Fit is only half.

00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:17.300
Intent comes from signals, and a
signal loses weight as it ages.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.567
A funding round fades slowly,
and a page visit fades fast.

00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:24.400
Hard disqualifiers, like a competitor
or a current customer, cap the score.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.233
Negative behavior, like an unsubscribe
or a hard bounce, pulls it down.

00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:31.633
That logic is built in, and you
tune the weights and thresholds.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.467
Step three, the buying committee.
Deals are bought by groups,

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.867
so the committee model
says which roles you need,

00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:38.400
and the score reads that coverage.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.533
If the champion or the economic buyer
is missing, the verdict says so.

00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:43.433
Unknown coverage never counts as zero.

00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:46.500
Customer types say how an enterprise
account differs from a self serve one,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.433
each with its own journey.

00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:50.000
Buying roles live in Settings,
Brand and compliance.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.000
And customer types are a card in setup.

00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:54.667
Step four, draft it from your
best customers. Ask your agent:

00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:58.233
"Draft an ICP scoring model from
our best customers and propose it".

00:01:58.233 --> 00:02:01.900
It proposes weights with the evidence
for each and files a ChangeSet.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.067
A different person
approves, and someone signs.

00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:07.033
Until then, engines keep using
the signed model, or the default.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.967
Step five, test it on a real account.
In the console: Engines, Score.

00:02:10.967 --> 00:02:11.800
Pick a company and qualify it.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.733
The verdict shows which fit criteria
matched and which signals counted,

00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:16.667
with how far each has decayed.

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:20.200
It also shows any cap or negative
that applied, and committee coverage.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.667
The Hot list shows the same
reasoning in a why drawer.

00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:26.100
If a score looks wrong, flag it. A
rep flag becomes a learning input.

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:28.267
Or ask your agent: "Preview the rescore,

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:30.400
then score account Example
Logistics and explain it.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.467
Tell me which environment you read".

00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:35.167
The preview shows how tiers
would move, and changes nothing.

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:37.533
Watch for this: an
unsigned draft is ignored,

00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:39.467
so test in dev once it's signed there.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:43.333
One note on Dry Mode. Scoring sends
nothing, so it runs in any mode.

00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:46.300
What Dry Mode holds is what leaves
the building: a CRM write back,

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.767
a routing notification
or an outreach message

00:02:48.767 --> 00:02:51.100
stays a draft until that engine goes live.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.333
And scores are only as good
as the data under them.

00:02:53.333 --> 00:02:57.100
On sample data you get sample scores,
and gtm doctor tells you what's live.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:01.167
Step six, let outcomes improve it. In
the console: Engines, Score, Learning.

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:04.667
Each week the engine measures which
criteria predicted replies, meetings,

00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:06.900
opportunities and wins, and
proposes a new model version.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.300
Weights move only a limited step per
run, and only on strong evidence.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.500
By default it only reports. A person
other than the proposer approves,

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.467
and then the organization is rescored.

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:19.067
A new organization shows
insufficient data at first.

00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:22.100
Or ask your agent: "Propose a
scoring update from outcomes".

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:23.033
The skeptic's line:

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:26.533
a model that rewrites itself will
drift where I can't see. It can't.

00:03:26.533 --> 00:03:28.567
Learning proposes, and a person approves.

00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:30.267
Weights move a limited step per run.

00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:34.000
The old version stays in history,
and a rollback is one proposal away.

00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:37.533
In one pass: Define who you sell
to and the weights that decide fit.

00:03:37.533 --> 00:03:40.533
Add the committee. Test on a real
account, and read the reasons.

00:03:40.533 --> 00:03:42.967
Then let outcomes
propose the next version.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.900
You now have a fit model you
can read, test and improve,

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.500
with two people on every change.
Test it on one real account today.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.567
After this comes journeys,
pipelines and revenue truth.
